Check the command scheduler against one-at-a-time processing

tests/carry_over_property_test.rs checks PendingPlays and the voice rule
against a sequential model, but its executor re-implements the
scheduler's routing, so a wiring mistake in src/main.rs (a voice hold
not released, a cancelled play started, a wrong reply) would get past
it. This test drives the real CommandScheduler instead.

Each generated script mixes plays (voices A to C or none, ids x and y
or none, two shared files over a real in-test HTTP server, about a
fifth of them answered 404, some with their own speed, volume or start
position), speed (including a negative speed with pitch correction),
volume, seek, stop, voice_stop, voice_fade_out, voice_volume, stopall,
and points where a chosen loading play's load ends. The scheduled run
submits the JSON to CommandScheduler with real loads and takes each
load's result where the script says, so loads end in any order. The
reference run sends the same commands one at a time through
handle_command: each play starts at once, and a play whose load fails
in the scheduled run plays until the point its load ended there. Both
runs finish fade-outs at once after a stop, so a stopped sound is gone
in both.

The runs must agree on every sound left playing (speed, volume, voice
volume, position), every named voice's volume, and every command's
outcome (success, 404, 400). Every play must be answered, and the load
of a cancelled play must start nothing. Coverage counters make the run
fail if no script answered a command that reached only loading plays,
set the volume of a voice held open by a loading play, cancelled a
play, failed a load, or started a play that commands arrived during
the load of.

64 cases by default, about 20 s in a debug build;
SCHEDULER_SCRIPT_CASES raises it. Each of these mutations fails it with
a shrunk script: speed not carried; voice_stop not cancelling; a failed
load keeping its voice; a command that reached only loading plays
answered "not found".

Validation: cargo fmt --check and cargo clippy --all-targets -D
warnings clean; the binary's tests 98 passed; the new test also passed
at 300 and 1000 cases.

